## STEP 1: SETTING UP THE RASPBERRY PI FOR THE FIRST TIME

> **What you'll do:** Write the operating system (OS) to the SD card, pre-configure login credentials and WiFi, then boot the Raspberry Pi for the first time — all without connecting a monitor or keyboard.

> **What you need for this step:**
> - Your laptop
> - The Micro SD card (32 GB or larger)
> - The SD card reader
> - Raspberry Pi Imager (downloaded from [raspberrypi.com/software](https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/))
> - Your current WiFi name and password

---

### Part A: Install Raspberry Pi Imager

1. On your **laptop**, go to https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/
2. Download and install **Raspberry Pi Imager** for your operating system (Windows/Mac)
3. Open Raspberry Pi Imager after installation

---

### Part B: Flash the Operating System to the SD Card

> **What does "flash" mean?** It means writing the operating system onto the SD card so the Raspberry Pi can boot from it — like installing Windows on a new computer, but for the Pi.

4. Insert the **Micro SD card** into the **SD card reader**, then plug the reader into your laptop

5. In Raspberry Pi Imager, you'll see three buttons. Click them in order:

   **5a. Choose Device:**
   - Click **"Choose Device"**
   - Select **Raspberry Pi 5**

   **5b. Choose OS:**
   - Click **"Choose OS"**
   - Select **Raspberry Pi OS (64-bit)** — this should be the first/top option
   - ⚠️ Make sure it says **64-bit**. Do NOT choose the 32-bit version.

   **5c. Choose Storage:**
   - Click **"Choose Storage"**
   - Select your SD card (it will show something like "Generic MassStorageClass USB Drive" or similar)
   - ⚠️ **Double-check** you selected the SD card, not your laptop's hard drive!

6. Click **"Next"**

---

### Part C: Configure the Pi's Settings (IMPORTANT)

> **Why do this now?** Since we're running the Pi without a monitor (headless), we need to pre-configure the username, password, WiFi, and SSH access now — otherwise we'll have no way to connect to it after it boots.

7. A popup will ask **"Would you like to apply OS customisation settings?"** — click **"Edit Settings"**

8. **In the "General" tab**, fill in the following:

   | Setting | What to Enter | Explanation |
   |---|---|---|
   | **Set hostname** | `trio` | This is the Pi's name on the network. You'll use `trio.local` to connect to it. |
   | **Set username and password** | Username: `pi`, Password: `raspberrypi` | These are the login credentials for the Pi. You'll need them every time you connect. |
   | **Configure wireless LAN** | Your WiFi name and password | Enter the WiFi you're currently connected to. The Pi will connect to this WiFi when it boots. |
   | **Set locale settings** | Your timezone and keyboard layout | Choose your correct timezone (e.g., Asia/Manila) |

9. **Click the "Services" tab**:
   - Check ✅ **"Enable SSH"**
   - Select **"Use password authentication"**

   > **What is SSH?** SSH (Secure Shell) lets you control the Pi remotely from your laptop's terminal — like a remote desktop, but text-based. This is how you'll run commands on the Pi without plugging in a monitor.

10. Click **"Save"**

11. Click **"Yes"** to apply the settings

12. Click **"Yes"** to confirm erasing the SD card

13. **Wait** for the write and verification to complete. This takes about 5–10 minutes.
    - You'll see a progress bar. Don't unplug the SD card until it's done.
    - When finished, you'll see **"Write Successful"**

---

### Part D: Boot the Raspberry Pi

14. **Safely eject** the SD card from your laptop (use "Safely Remove Hardware" on Windows)

15. **Insert the SD card** into the Raspberry Pi 5
    - The SD card slot is on the bottom of the Pi
    - Push it in gently until it clicks

16. **Connect the USB-C power supply** to the Pi
    - The Pi will boot automatically when power is connected
    - You'll see a small green LED blinking — this means it's working

17. **Wait 2–3 minutes**
    - On the first boot, the Pi expands the filesystem and applies your settings
    - It will also connect to the WiFi you configured in Step 8
    - Don't unplug it during this time

> **✅ Checkpoint:** The Pi is now running and connected to your WiFi. You can't see anything because there's no monitor — that's normal. In the next step, you'll connect to it from your laptop using SSH.

---

### What If Something Goes Wrong?

| Problem | What To Do |
|---|---|
| Pi's green LED doesn't blink at all | The SD card might not be inserted properly, or the flash failed. Re-do Part B. |
| Pi has a steady red LED only | Power supply issue. Make sure you're using a USB-C cable rated for 5V/5A. |
| You made a typo in the WiFi password | You'll need to re-flash the SD card (start from Part B again) or connect the Pi to a monitor temporarily to fix it. |
